enturies ago with looking at critical  studies of religion of very many kinds  anthropological psychological  sociological historical and literary and  it seems to me that those actually do  show that the situation with respect to  religious belief is not only a bit worse  than it is in other domains but a lot  worse now the quays the case of values  to which you alluded is I think a  particularly interesting one and and  actually quite a troubling one and that  is that so I think I can fight a very  clearly for a more a marked difference  between the scientific case say where I  think when one looks more closely the  symmetry can be broken and the religious  case will and when one looks more  closely the symmetry seems to be if  anything deepened by by the closer  looking but the case of values is really  hard and I want to say more about that  on on Thursday and maybe your question  will come back with renewed vengeance at  that point I want to try to assimilate  actually the values case more to the  scientific case and to cut it off from  from the religious case I think that  just to give you a foretaste  I think that we are still struggling to  and this is a very dewy end point do we  thought that something remarkable had  happened in the 17th century that people  had then discovered a method that could  be used for finding things out about the  physical world about nature and that the  the imperative task for philosophy in  the early 20th century and I think you  would say in the early 21st century too  was to do the same sort of thing with  respect to the human and the the moral  sciences I think that's absolutely right  I think we're still struggling towards a  method for for finding out and reaching  consensus on matters of values and  breaking the simple the symmetry here  but it's still a work in progress and I  will be talking about that on Thursday  but thank you those are excellent  questions and I intend in the written  version to say much more about that as  as you rightly suggested
 

